# Define one typed diagnostic envelope

## Scope and existing evidence

Represent field, object, page, source, glyph, collision, capability, renderer,
and hardware errors with stable codes and actionable locations.

Current executable evidence now implements the versioned
`atrament.diagnostic/1` backend domain model. Handshake incompatibility and
resource-limit results preserve their application result classes while carrying
shared diagnostic sets with stable codes, severity, blocking disposition,
semantic locations, typed evidence, remediation, operation binding, and
explicit set completeness. The browser admits the same namespace and
completeness metadata; handshake mismatch projection also requires the reported
expected identity to equal the browser's current value for that exact dimension.

The localhost adapter requires exactly one route-specific application
diagnostic for the current singleton handshake-mismatch and draft-resource
response schemas.
Empty, multi-item, incomplete, advisory, wrong-severity, wrong-location
identity, malformed-location, wrong-remediation, contextualized,
cross-operation, or wrong-code diagnostics fail as `invalid_diagnostic`.
Handshake projection also requires one
`RequiredVersion` evidence item that exactly matches the reported dimension and
expected identity, while the result's observed identity must equal the version
actually presented for that dimension and differ from the expected identity.
Draft resource projection requires one byte-limit observation whose observed
count equals the rejected request body and exceeds its reported maximum, so
transport neither invents, truncates, nor relabels application evidence.

Layout now provides the first accepted-revision producer beyond handshake and
session draft: fixed-region overflow emits stable blocking diagnostics with
semantic object/page locations, typed boundary and physical-length evidence,
operation binding, and complete-set semantics. Their current opaque object,
page, and revision identities use one in-process Debug-shaped projection only;
no browser, CLI, MCP, or file-format identity encoding is admitted by this
evidence. A layout-only Export preflight preserves those diagnostics through
that same internal projection rather than interpreting prose.

A transport-neutral semantic-command Apply producer now exists, but its typed
results do not yet carry `DiagnosticSet`. The shared envelope now reserves the
stable `atrament.semantic-command.precondition-rejected` code and typed local
precondition condition/failure evidence for family, kind, owner, target,
editability, and exact-value checks. Domain fixtures prove the code, Semantic
Validate binding, command/object/field locations, and typed evidence shape
without inventing adapter prose.

Complete external diagnostic identity projection remains open because accepted
semantic identities are intentionally opaque and command identities are still
generic in the pre-normalization batch. No stable diagnostic identity text or
context binding exists yet, so the application must not leak Debug formatting to
satisfy the fixture. Render, full Export, and Plan producers also remain open,
and CLI/MCP parity cannot yet prove cross-capability semantics.

The browser diagnostic metadata guard now refuses arrays in both the root
set and individual items, even when a JavaScript caller attaches properties
that mimic object fields. A red-first fixture proved the prior guard accepted
these non-object shapes, contrary to the independent fail-closed reference.
This is local parser-shape correctness, not a claim of network exploitability.

The same browser object-shape rule now also applies to handshake response and
version records and to resource-limit response roots. Their own independent
fail-closed reference tests already rejected arrays; red-first regressions
confirm the former implementation incorrectly accepted augmented JavaScript
arrays as compatible handshakes or known draft resource-limit errors. These
shape guards do not change how valid JSON object payloads are interpreted.

The browser diagnostic-set adapter also rejects holes and inherited indexed
diagnostic items. A red-first regression showed that `Array.every` skipped a
sparse slot and accepted metadata without the required item record. The parser
now checks own occupied slots, including arrays constructed in-process; this
does not imply such sparse shapes can arrive through JSON transport.

The browser diagnostic, handshake and draft parsers also require own data
properties for protocol fields instead of accepting prototype-inherited values.
Red-first JavaScript fixtures proved that ordinary objects with inherited
version, completeness, item code, handshake dimensions, or resource-limit error
were previously misclassified as admitted metadata. A null-prototype object
with genuine own fields still passes; the ordinary JSON response flow is
unchanged. This is local shape validation, not evidence of remote exploitation.

Browser diagnostic, handshake, and draft response adapters now require own
*data* properties on the admitted protocol fields. A red-first test for each
adapter showed that own accessor getters could previously supply otherwise
valid identities and be invoked during validation, despite the earlier
prototype-field guard. Descriptor-only reads now reject those shapes without
executing the getters; ordinary decoded JSON remains accepted. This is an
in-process parser integrity rule, not a claim of remote code execution.

Browser diagnostic-set metadata now also bounds the number of decoded items it
will inspect to 128, before walking array slots. A red-first fixture showed
that 129 otherwise valid entries were previously admitted. An oversized
in-process sparse-array proxy fixture proves the parser rejects by declared
length without invoking any slot descriptors; the exact 128-item boundary
remains admitted.

A separate red-first proxy fixture also showed ordinary `items.length` access
could invoke in-process getter traps. Reading the array's own data descriptor
now avoids that getter path for admitted metadata. Current localhost handshake
and draft responses still emit only one application diagnostic each. This is
CPU-work bounding for the browser parser, not evidence that the backend can
transmit huge diagnostic arrays or that downstream schemas are complete.

A follow-up red-first regression showed that returning the original diagnostic
items array after validation still allowed the route-specific handshake and
draft consumers to invoke input-array Proxy getters when reading item zero.
The parser now copies at most 128 admitted item references into a fresh array,
so subsequent array-slot changes to the caller's collection do not affect
transport classification. Item object fields still use descriptor-only reads;
this does not turn arbitrary in-process objects into network-origin evidence.

The browser parser now copies each admitted diagnostic item into an owned
record, not merely an owned items array. Red-first handshake and draft tests
showed that rereading a retained in-process Proxy's `code` descriptor could
throw after initial admission; a direct parser test showed mutable item fields
could change the earlier result. Snapshotting own data descriptors once avoids
both paths, retains ordinary JSON metadata, and refuses accessor-only fields.

Each item is limited to 128 own string fields in addition to the existing
128-item collection cap. This is a browser in-process integrity and work bound,
not remote JSON security evidence or complete diagnostic schema validation.

The public browser diagnostic, handshake, and draft-response entrypoints now
classify in-process descriptor failures and revoked Proxy inputs as invalid,
rather than propagating exceptions from an otherwise guarded read. Red-first
regressions exercised throwing root fields, a nested version record, indexed
diagnostic slots and revoked objects; ordinary JSON fixtures remain unchanged.
The rule applies only to these parsing results, not arbitrary JavaScript errors
or network-reachable Proxy payloads.
